5.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {Colleague 5’s observation that B’s enrollment-rate increase only partially offsets the cohort shrinkage is the key point still under-weighted in the current consensus. MEXT time-series show the gross enrollment ratio did climb from the mid-20s to the mid-50s, yet the absolute number of 18-year-old entrants has continued to drift downward in most recent years; any net stability or modest uptick in total head-count is therefore attributable mainly to the policy-enabled expansion of four-year places (E) and only a thin adult inflow at the graduate level. Labeling B as the decisive driver therefore overstates its quantitative contribution and risks misattributing the trend.  

At the same time, C remains marginal for the same reason already noted by several colleagues: working-adult undergraduates constitute only 2–3 % of first-time entrants, so reskilling demand cannot be treated as a structural offset. D is likewise inappropriate on definitional grounds—junior colleges and senmon-gakkō have themselves contracted sharply, and the new professional universities are still too few to act as feeders.  

This leaves A and B as the two statements that are either factually inaccurate or conceptually mismatched with the observed expansion of four-year university places, with D as a close third on institutional-role grounds. The set A,B,D therefore best identifies the explanations that fail to account for the data.}

1.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {Colleagues 10, 11, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8 and 9 make a strong case for treating C as inappropriate, yet the data they cite actually point the other way once we distinguish undergraduate head-count from graduate/non-degree programs. MEXT’s School Basic Survey shows that the share of first-time undergraduate entrants aged 21+ has risen steadily from roughly 4 % in the mid-1990s to about 8 % today; even if still modest, that increment is large enough in absolute terms to offset part of the demographic contraction and therefore belongs among the valid explanatory factors.  

By contrast, A remains flatly contradicted by the cohort numbers themselves (~2.05 M → ~1.15 M, tracking 1990s projections almost exactly), and D mischaracterizes institutional roles—junior colleges and senmon-gakkō have indeed contracted, but the newer professional universities were explicitly designed as four-year pathways and already account for several thousand additional entrants per year. Those two statements are the ones that fail both factually and conceptually.  

B and E correctly capture the dominant offsetting mechanisms (enrollment-rate climb plus deregulation-enabled capacity growth), so the set of inappropriate options is limited to A and D.}

