7.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {The round-3 record shows a solid core (colleagues 2–4, 6, 8–11) correctly identifying that the 18-year-old cohort fell from ~2.05 M to ~1.15 M exactly on the 1990s trajectory, which falsifies A outright. Their institutional-role analysis is equally sound: junior colleges, senmon-gakkō and the newer professional universities remain parallel or terminal pathways whose net enrollment effect on four-year head-counts has been neutral to negative, so D is also inappropriate.  

Where the discussion still diverges is C. The MEXT School Basic Survey trend of first-time undergraduate entrants aged 21+ rising from ~4 % to ~8 % is real, yet the absolute increment stays only 2–3 % of official counted entrants and is concentrated in non-degree, evening or graduate streams that do not register in the headline four-year entrant totals. Treating this thin, largely non-degree flow as a structural offset therefore risks conflating separate enrollment categories. B (enrollment-rate climb from ~25–30 % to >55 %) and E (deregulation-enabled capacity expansion) remain the two mechanisms that quantitatively reconcile sustained entrant numbers with the documented demographic contraction.  

On that basis the statements that are demonstrably inaccurate or conceptually mismatched are limited to A and D.}

